## Create a builder

A project builder defines what a Moth project can use and turns its compiled
assemblies into application artefacts. A game builder can provide engine
packages and emit Wasm. A web builder can provide HTML helpers and assemble
browser output. Both use the same compiler and build services.

Use the public `moth::builder` API for this work. The four main values are
`BuilderDefinition`, `CompiledProject`, `BuildContext` and `BuildArtifacts`.
`ProjectBuilder` connects them through two methods:

- `definition` describes the packages, configuration and capabilities available
  to the project's source.
- `build` consumes a successfully compiled project and requests the artefacts
  that implement the builder's application model.

The build driver calls `definition` before configuration or source compilation.
It retains the resulting immutable definition for the build. It calls `build`
only after the required semantic compilation succeeds. A builder doesn't
sequence parsing, type checking or borrow analysis itself.

### A Wasm package builder

This builder compiles the application's explicit project package facade to
Wasm. It also makes a Moth source package and a Rust host package available.
The example package has no output-relative resource strings.

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    use std::path::PathBuf;

    use moth::builder::{
        BuildArtifacts, BuildContext, BuilderDefinition, CompiledProject,
        Error, HostPackage, ProjectBuilder, SourcePackage,
    };

    #[moth::host_function]
    fn has_prefix(text: &str, prefix: &str) -> bool {
        text.starts_with(prefix)
    }

    struct GameBuilder {
        package_root: PathBuf,
    }

    impl ProjectBuilder for GameBuilder {
        fn definition(&self) -> Result<BuilderDefinition, Error> {
            BuilderDefinition::new("game")
                .source_package(
                    SourcePackage::new("@game")
                        .from_dir(&self.package_root),
                )
                .host_package(
                    HostPackage::new("@platform")
                        .function(moth::host_binding!(has_prefix)),
                )
                .moth_templates()
                .markdown()
                .finish()
        }

        fn build(
            &self,
            project: &CompiledProject,
            context: &mut BuildContext,
        ) -> Result<BuildArtifacts, Error> {
            let package = project.require_package()?;
            let wasm = context.compile_wasm(package)?;

            let mut output = BuildArtifacts::new();
            output.add("game.wasm", wasm)?;
            Ok(output)
        }
    }
]

`require_package` returns the owner-bound assembly for the authored project
facade. A missing facade produces a builder diagnostic. The facade selects its
public surface through normal Moth visibility rules. It has no implicit
`start`, page output or access to private declarations in other modules.

`compile_wasm` compiles the selected assembly and its reachable dependencies.
The result retains the import, export and runtime contract needed by an
embedding host. It doesn't make every source module a separate Wasm binary,
create a WIT component or execute the program.

`output.add` records an artefact under a relative output path. It preserves the
artefact's associated contracts and rejects conflicting records. It doesn't
write the final file. The central output owner validates destinations,
materialises resource bytes and applies manifest-based cleanup.

An assembly containing browser-only operations cannot be compiled for a native
Wasm host merely by calling this method. Target validation reports the
unsupported reachable operation. Resource-bearing assemblies also require the
output context described in Assembly and runtime output.

### Invoke the build driver

Use `BuildRequest` to supply the project location and per-build options. Keep
installed builder package locations separate from the application source root.
This example obtains the package directory relative to the Rust crate that
contains the builder:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    use moth::builder::{BuildRequest, build_project};

    fn main() -> Result<(), moth::builder::Error> {
        let builder = GameBuilder {
            package_root: PathBuf::from(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR"))
                .join("moth/game"),
        };
        let request = BuildRequest::new("game-project");
        let result = build_project(&builder, request)?;

        result.write_outputs()?;
        Ok(())
    }
]

The Moth package directory contains its normal package facade and source files.
When distributing a builder, install those files at a known location and pass
that location explicitly. An absolute installation path locates bytes. It is
not the package's semantic identity.

Building and writing are separate operations so an embedding tool can inspect
results before committing output. An unsuccessful compilation never supplies a
partial `CompiledProject` to `build`. A failed build never authorises stale
output cleanup as though a replacement build had succeeded.

### Define once, consume through services

The fluent helpers construct one validated capability surface. `finish`
validates the complete registration and returns an immutable definition or a
structured error. Duplicate names, conflicting package roots, invalid defaults
and attempts to override compiler-owned facilities fail instead of replacing
an earlier registration.

The definition contains builder policy, not an independent compiler. Source
packages still use Moth semantic interfaces. Native host packages still use the
canonical binding machinery. Helpers don't introduce parallel type tables,
configuration parsers or copies of compiler result stores.

Builder identity is a stable declared identity used for ownership and
compatibility. It isn't a Rust type name, filesystem path or enum entry that
requires editing the compiler for each builder. Source aliases don't change
package or output ownership.

One selected numeric profile governs directly linked Moth code. The
`numeric_profile` definition helper selects that profile before configuration
values are materialised. Build mode and physical target selection don't change
it. Compiler-owned names and mandatory Core facilities remain authoritative.

### Build-time code and runtime code

The Rust builder uses the compiler as a library. The running application uses a
host runtime and its installed bindings. These are separate responsibilities,
even when one executable contains both.

Declaring `has_prefix` makes its interface available during compilation.
Installing its adapter in the application's Wasm host makes it callable during
execution. Registering an interface alone neither installs a native function in
a browser nor sends a request to a server. A target without an implementation
rejects reachable calls to that binding.

A project builder reuses compiler backends. Writing a new backend is a different
job that consumes validated compiler IR through its own handoff. Ordinary
builder code uses assemblies and services rather than public access to HIR,
type environments or analysis side tables.
